msgid "Manage vocabularies"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Install vocabularies on this computer, link values to their terms, and "
"keep a copy of the terms in the file.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A controlled vocabulary gives a word a code that holds in every language:"
" the material written *limestone* in one study and *calcare* in another "
"becomes the same term. EMStudio treats vocabularies as a document treats "
"its fonts. They are installed on the computer, once, and shared by every "
"study and by EM Tools. A file declares which vocabularies and versions it"
" uses, and keeps the label of each value as it was written, so it opens "
"everywhere, also where a vocabulary is missing. When the licence allows "
"it, the file can also carry a copy of the few terms it uses."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"All of this happens in one page, ``Settings… ▸ Vocabularies``, and in the"
" **Inspector** of a value."
msgstr ""

msgid "Install a vocabulary"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Settings, the Vocabularies page: a vocabulary installed on this computer,"
" the buttons to install, the alignments, and the vocabularies the open "
"file uses."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Open ``Settings… ▸ Vocabularies``. In the **Inspector**, beside a value "
"linked to a term, **Edit vocabulary…** opens the same page; the command "
"search (:kbd:`F3`) finds it as **Vocabularies…**."
msgstr ""

msgid "In the section **Install**, choose where the vocabulary comes from:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**From the central repository…**: the catalogue of the Extended Matrix "
"vocabularies. Every vocabulary it lists is installed or brought up to "
"date."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**From the StratiGraph node…**: the catalogue of your node. The button is"
" there when a node is configured."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**From a file (.emvocab.zip)…**: a vocabulary package, or a community "
"package whose vocabularies go to the vocabularies of this computer."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A message confirms each vocabulary installed. Its card appears under "
"**Installed on this computer**: its title, then its code, version, date, "
"languages, tier and licence, and the attribution its licence asks for."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Look for updates** compares the installed vocabularies with the central"
" catalogue and installs the newer versions. A new version goes beside the"
" old one: the card lists the **Versions** side by side, each with who "
"brought it, and a file keeps reading the version it declares."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A vocabulary in tier C has a licence that does not let it travel: only "
"its card is installed. **Download locally** fetches it from its source "
"and keeps it on this computer only. When the source asks you to accept "
"its terms first, press **Terms of the source…**, download the file from "
"that page, then choose it with **Download locally: choose the file…**; "
"its fingerprint is checked."
msgstr ""

msgid "Link a value to a term"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Select a property whose field takes its values from a vocabulary, for "
"example the **Material** of SU001. In the **Inspector**, under the value,"
" the sign ◇ and **not controlled: text only** say that the value is a "
"plain word."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Link to a vocabulary…**. The window **Link to a vocabulary** "
"repeats the value and lists the candidates that the installed "
"vocabularies propose, each marked **same label** or **close**, with its "
"vocabulary and version."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choose the term the word means."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Choose **This value only**, or **All … values written** with that word, "
"which says how many values share it."
msgstr ""

msgid "Press **Link**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The word stays the one written; the term gives it its code. The whole "
"link is one step of ``Edit ▸ Undo``. The Inspector now shows ◆ with the "
"label of the term in the language of the interface and its vocabulary; "
"its tooltip gives the code of the term and the version."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The same signs appear beside the properties in the **Outliner** and in "
"the Matrix: ◆ for a value linked to a term, ◆? for a term whose "
"vocabulary is not installed on this computer (the saved label is shown), "
"◇ for a value that is only text. The **Warnings** sheet of the "
"**Tabular** window lists the values that are not controlled and those "
"whose vocabulary is missing, with **Link to a vocabulary…** and "
"**Install…** beside them."
msgstr ""

msgid "Read what the file uses, and carry a copy of its terms"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In ``Settings… ▸ Vocabularies``, the section **This file** lists, for the"
" active file, each vocabulary it uses, with its versions and how many "
"terms."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A vocabulary that is not installed here is marked **not installed here "
"(the saved labels are shown)**, with **Install** beside it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Tick **Copy the terms used into the file** to make the file carry, at the"
" next save, a copy of the terms it uses, with all their languages. Only "
"vocabularies in tiers A and B are copied, because their licence lets them"
" travel. Unticked, the file declares its vocabularies without copying "
"them, as a document names its fonts."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The message that confirms the choice offers **Undo** for a few seconds. "
"The choice is saved with the file."
msgstr ""

msgid "Show labels in more languages"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An alignment says that a term of one vocabulary is the same as a term of "
"another, so that a label can be shown in the languages of both. Only the "
"rows a person has verified are used. In the section **Alignments**, press"
" **Install alignments from a file (.yaml, .json)…**; each alignment is "
"listed with its rows and how many are verified, and **Remove** takes it "
"away."
msgstr ""

msgid "Remove a vocabulary"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"On its card, **Remove** takes the vocabulary off this computer, and "
"**Remove** followed by a version takes away that version only. A file "
"that uses it still opens, with the labels saved in its values. A "
"vocabulary brought by a community package stays when the package is "
"removed, like a font left by a program: its card says that no package "
"brings it any more."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Vocabularies installed once on the computer and used by every study; "
"values of the study linked to terms that hold in every language; and a "
"file that declares what it uses, opens everywhere, and, if you wish, "
"carries the terms it needs."
msgstr ""

